// kindle — a light that spreads. funideas §6's other neighbour word: not
// "every row an oscillator that entrains" (`motes`, `sync`) but "a thing one
// row has and passes on" (`infect`).
//
// Nothing here decides that a front should travel. Three independent facts
// do, and the picture is what they add up to:
//
//   a few rows are always alight       (the sources, from `row.seed`)
//   a row catches from whoever is near  (`infect`)
//   every row goes out on its own       (`relax 0`)
//
// The last two are the whole system. `spread` against `heal` is an epidemic
// threshold: below it a source glows alone in a small halo, above it the
// light crosses the cloud and keeps crossing. That number is worth turning
// by hand, which is exactly why `infect` does not own it — recovery is a
// separate fact and `relax` was already the word for it.
//
// ⚠ `heal` is named for the epidemic and reads BACKWARDS for an artist: it is
// the knob that makes the light GO OUT.
//
// ⚠ NOT the same file as matryoshka's `kernels/kindle.rill`, which is this
// kernel plus a colour curve. If you were told "tune kindle", check which repo
// you opened.
//
//   row.u0  lit     0 dark → 1 alight. This is what a renderer reads.
//   row.u1  source  approaching 1 for the few rows that are never out, else 0.
//                   Never exactly 1 — see the line that writes it.
//   row.u3  crowd   how many are in reach — written for a reader, nothing here
//                   consumes it
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`, so each of these needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/kindle.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.reach =<radius, cells>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.spread=<per s, toward the brightest neighbour>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.heal  =<per s, back toward dark>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.stoke =<per s, a source relighting itself>
//
// ⚠ A `relax` RATE has a ceiling: a step that would close more than the whole
// gap is refused, so any rate above 1/dt refuses rather than clamping.

describe kindle
    "A light that spreads: a scattered few rows are never out, every row catches from the brightest thing in reach, and every row dims on its own — so a bright front travels across the cloud with darkness closing in behind it."

spawn
perish

// The sources: `row.seed` is a per-row uniform in [0, 1), so `mul n | sub n-1`
// keeps the top 1/n of them — here 1/60, about 1.7% — and `clamp` floors the
// rest at zero. The rule is the PAIR: to light 5% instead, write
// `mul 20 | sub 19`. It leaves a soft edge rather than a hard set, which is
// why `row.u1` approaches 1 without reaching it.
row.seed | mul 60 | sub 59 | clamp 0 1 | write row.u1

// Who is close, and catch it from whoever among them has most.
near plane.drift.@self.k.reach | write row.u3
infect row.u0 plane.drift.@self.k.spread

// Everybody goes out, all the time. Against the line above, this is the
// threshold; on its own it is why a front has a back.
row.u0 | relax 0 plane.drift.@self.k.heal | write row.u0 add

// And a source holds its own light up, so there is always something to
// catch. Gated on `row.u1`, so it is the sources' line and nobody else's.
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.stoke | mul row.u1 | write row.u0 add
